\caption(The reopening values come from an actual native event, and the goal path comes from independent plan checking. At larger budgets DFS can select longer valid routes, as the executed table shows.)
\alttext(Three boxes compare state 6 at depth 2 with one remaining step, state 6 at depth 1 with two remaining steps, and the certified suffix 6 to 1 to 7. Budget 3 now finds the three-action plan.)

> State dominance gives safe pruning, not shortest-path search

For budget K, let d(s) be the smallest depth at which a nonterminal state s has
been expanded. A later arrival at depth d is dominated if d≥d(s): any suffix
available with K−d steps is also available with K−d(s) steps. If d<d(s), that
reason no longer applies and the state is reopened. Goal testing comes first,
then the depth limit, then the dominance test, so a goal exactly at K is accepted.
A non-goal cutoff state is not inserted into the expansion map.

Soundness follows by induction: each selected action passes its preconditions,
its transition preserves precisely the undeleted facts plus additions, and the
returned terminal state passes the goal conjunction within K steps. Bounded
completeness follows from the dominance argument: a pruned occurrence can be
replaced by a no-deeper expansion with at least the same available suffix budget.
Following such replacements along any feasible bounded path cannot remove its
goal opportunity. The finite state space and strictly improving stored depths
make the search terminate, subject to available memory and representable counters.
This does not imply a shortest returned plan. DFS stops at its first successful
route in operator order; the table’s budgets 4 and 5 can select four- and five-step
plans even though a three-step plan exists.

The correction preserves the legacy record layouts, default 32-step budget,
operator order and add-wins semantics. Against 397686 frozen cases and an independent
breadth-first oracle, it recovers two old false negatives. One formerly successful
case selects a different valid route, three steps instead of two; the other 397683
results are identical. This is an explicit consequence of correcting pruning,
not a promise of shortestness. The original one-action example is unchanged.

> A small description can require exponentially many applications

The native binary-counter family has b operators. Operator k requires all lower
bits, clears those bits and sets bit k. Already-set bits can cause backward
transitions because the model has no negative preconditions. Input order tests
low bits first; equal-or-deeper repeated states are pruned.

From zero, the first successful path visits integer masks 0,1,2,…,G with
G=2^(b−1), the first state holding the highest goal bit. An applicable carry at a
previously unset bit increases the numeric state by exactly 1; an already-set bit
does not increase it. Thus no plan can reach G in fewer than G applications, and
the observed sequential path attains that lower bound. This is a property of
this constructed family, not a general shortestness guarantee.

For a budget of exactly G, the native counters satisfy

$$
\text{selected length}=G,\quad \text{expanded}=G,\quad
\text{operator checks}=2G-1,\quad \text{visits}=2G,\quad
\text{peak frames}=G+1,\qquad G=2^{b-1}.
$$
\alttext(The counter-family solution length and expanded states are G; actual operator checks are two G minus one, visits are two G, and the largest heap stack has G plus one frames, where G is two to the number of bits minus one.)

At state i, search tests one plus the number of trailing one bits before the
next advancing carry. Summing those tests over 0≤i<G gives 2G−1. Every tested carry
is applicable, but earlier backward/self-loop children fail; there are G−1 such
backtracks. Each child gives one visit, in addition to the root. On the last
non-goal state, the b−1 backward children reach the exact depth limit and are
counted as cutoffs before the repeat check; the other G−b are repeat prunes.
All these counters exclude trace capture, copies and hashing internals.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/planner_scaling.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/general_problem_solver.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 std::vector<double>bits_x,length_y,visits_y;std::print("| fact bits | plan length | visits | operator checks | repeat prunes | depth cutoffs | peak heap frames |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 std::size_t last_states=0;bool certified=false;
 for(std::size_t bits:std::array<std::size_t,10>{1,2,3,4,6,8,10,12,14,16}){const auto goal=std::uint64_t{1}<<(bits-1);const auto a=dm::algorithms::planning_binary_counter(bits);const auto r=dm::algorithms::general_problem_solver_run(0,goal,a,goal);const auto c=dm::algorithms::check_planning_plan(0,goal,a,r.action_indices,goal+1);const auto&w=r.work;
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",bits,r.action_indices.size(),w.visits,w.operator_checks,w.repeat_prunes,w.depth_cutoffs,w.peak_stack);bits_x.push_back(bits);length_y.push_back(std::log2(double(r.action_indices.size())));visits_y.push_back(std::log2(double(w.visits)));last_states=c.states.size();certified=c.applicable&&c.reaches_goals&&c.final_state==goal;
  if(!r.result.found||!certified||r.action_indices.size()!=goal||w.operator_checks!=2*goal-1||w.visits!=2*goal)throw std::runtime_error("counter-family closed cost");
 }
 std::print("\nLargest checked prefix states {}; independent goal certificate {}; the search uses heap frames rather than recursive C++ calls.\n",last_states,certified);
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(bits_x,length_y,"Selected length",{37,99,235},2).points(bits_x,length_y,"",{37,99,235},5)
  .lines(bits_x,visits_y,"Actual visits",{220,38,38},2).points(bits_x,visits_y,"",{220,38,38},5).legend({"Selected length","Actual visits"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
  .title("Counter family: linear input description, exponential executed work").xlab("Number of fact bits and input operators").ylab("log2 positive executed count").y_limits(-1,20).size(1100,560).save_svg("build/planner_scaling.svg");
}
