// result_end
\caption(The sixth actual search event is finish, so cap 5 cannot return even this simple trace. Certificate admission counts the whole three-state proposal; high-bit effects and duplicate names are checked by indices.)
\alttext(Two panels show search event counts 6 observed,6 admitted and 5 rejected, then certificate counts 3 required,3 captured and 2 rejected. Computed controls verify ownership, high-bit add-wins and invalid-index precedence.)

Actual search events explain both successful plans and the corrected depth-limit
failure. Independent certificates separate executable actions from a found flag,
and operator indices preserve identity when names do not. The counter family
exposes exponential work despite a short description and a heap-based traversal.
These finite constructed domains directly test the planning model; an external
dataset is unnecessary, and none of the counts promises shortest plans or fast
execution for arbitrary fact systems.
